  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure centered near the Mid-Atlantic region this\nmorning is still in control our our weather. Today will be a \nrather decent day for most of the area. Skies will start out \nmostly sunny to partly cloudy but clouds will increase late in \nthe day ahead of an approaching cold front. Temperatures will \nreach the upper 70s to lower 80s this afternoon. \n\nA weak cold front approaching from the west will move into\nNorthwest Ohio this evening. A broken line of showers and\nthunderstorms are expected to develop along that cold front with\na few strong to severe storms possible, mainly for NWOH between\n6 pm and 11 pm. For the thermodynamics ahead the front,\neffective shear values will between 30-40 knots, MLCAPE values\nbetween 1000-1500 J/kg, and downdraft CAPE up to 1000 J/kg. The\nlatest high-res model guidance in good agreement with the\npotential of a few strong to severe storms with damaging winds\nup to 60 mph as the main threat followed a less threat of large\nhail up to the size of Quarters. The latest SPC severe weather\noutlook today has Lucas, Wood, Ottawa, and Hancock Counties in \na slight risk (level 2 of 5). There is a marginal severe storm \nrisk (level 1 of 5) from Wyandot County Ohio northeast to the \nLake Erie Islands this evening. As the convection tracks\neastward late this evening, it will move into a stable \natmosphere and quickly dissipate before it reaches the I-71 \ncorridor.\n\nThis slow moving front will be over NEOH and NWPA Thursday. \nScattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to redevelop \neast of I-71 midday through the early afternoon before it moves\nout of the area. Severe weather is not expected with any of the\nstorms on Thursday. A surface trough will southward across Lake\nErie Thursday evening through early Friday morning with a few \nrain showers possible near the lakeshore.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA stretch of much warmer and nice weather will start Friday as \nHigh pressure will builds southeastward across the Great Lakes\nand Ohio Valley. The overall weather pattern will transition\nfrom an upper level to upper level ridging this weekend.\nEventually an upper level ridge of high pressure will develop\nand stay over the Ohio Valley this weekend into early next week. \nThis weather pattern will favor above average temperature in the 80s \nto around 90 degrees. Overnight low temps will be in the 60s to \naround 70 degrees. By the next Monday night and Tuesday, a weak \nfrontal boundary will try to drift southward into our area and a \nreturn of chance POPs.",
